hey guys Greg knuckles here with string  theory calm back with the I believe  sixth installment of our daily Q&amp;A  series today's question is how fast can  I cut without unnecessarily increasing  the risk of losing muscle in the process  the reason I phrase it like that  unnecessarily increase the risk is  because when you're cutting there is  always a chance you're going to lose  some muscle in the process it does just  seem that some people naturally hang on  to muscle better when they're cutting  than others do and also plenty of  factors go into this right so are you  still lifting are you eating enough  protein  are you sleeping enough how much you  sleep can impact the ratio of lean mass  to fat mass loss so there's there's a  lot of factors here but essentially the  question I'm answering is above what  rate of weight loss do you dramatically  increase your odds of losing quite a bit  of lean mass when you're cutting so to  answer this question there are two major  factors the first factor is how big you  are and the second is how much body fat  you currently have there was a paper  published in 2005 by a researcher named  Albert and essentially what it set out  to do is see what the upper limit was on  how quickly body fat stores essentially  could liberate fat to be burned and lost  and in that paper the calculated rate  was right around 30 calories per day per  pound of body fat and I saw I think an  interview with the researcher a couple  years after that paper was published  where he said he reran the calculations  and it was more like 22 calories per  pound so I'm just gonna kind of split  the  difference and say about 25 calories per  pound and I'm also doing that because it  makes the math easier you'll see later  so okay  25 calories per pound so you need to  know two things again how big you are  and what your body fat percentage is  that tells you you know how much fat is  on your body and then once you know that  you can know how many calories that fat  can liberate on a per day basis and then  you can calculate a kind of that upper  limit range of weight-loss and so if you  wanted to do all of the steps of the  calculation it would be your body weight  times your body fat percentage times 25  calories per pound times seven days per  week divided by 3500 because there's  roughly 3,500 calories per pound of fat  tissue and then divide that by your  current body weight and that would tell  you the percentage of your body weight  you could lose per week what what you  should aim for is kind of an upper limit  but like I was saying going with 25  calories per pound makes that math much  much easier you can just take your  current body fat percentage and divide  it by 20 just simplifying a bunch of  those steps and that will give you the  percentage of your current body weight  per week that you should lose so body  fat percentage divided by 20 so if  you're 10 percent 10 divided by 20 that  gives you half a percent so you should  shoot to lose about half a percent of  body weight per week so if you're 200  pounds that would be a pound a week if  you're 20 percent body fat then you can  probably shoot to lose about 1 pound per  week so you know if you're 200 pounds 2  pounds per week if you're 30 percent you  can shoot to lose about one and a half  percent of your body weight per week so  if you're 200 pounds three pounds per  week so it's going the the absolute  amount of weight loss that's impacted by  how large you are and then the  percentage of your body weight per week  you're losing is impacted by what your  current body fat percentage is so no  surprise here the more body  yeah.the the higher body fat percentage  you have the faster you can cut  initially so you know if you're 30% one  and a half percent per week down to 10%  half a percent per week so that's going  to impact that and yeah it's it's  largely just based on how quickly her  your fat can can drop fat on a per pound  basis get it into your bloodstream so  you can burn it lose that weight so yeah  really really simple formula there and  again that's that's not taking into  account every single possible scenario  every single possible nuances are  training right are you eating enough  protein is your diet good are you  sleeping enough but just painting in  broad strokes here in general body fat  percentage divided by 20 that is roughly  the percent the percentage of your  current body weight that you can shoot  to lose per week just to make that  calculation nice and easy on you so yeah  and that's that's actually what I'm  using now for my cut obviously I still  have a long way to go but I'm down  almost 25 pounds from where I started  and that that feels pretty good and this  is this is what kind of the the guide  that I've been using for my cut as well  and to this point like I said I  definitely had plenty of fat to lose  when I started still do but my strength  has maintained and actually gone up a  little bit since I started the cut so  even though it has been fairly  aggressive by most standards but it's  it's very very close to kind of the  upper end of the range proposed by this  formula so just in my own experience I  think it works pretty well yeah and it  is just a nifty easy little calculation  you can do for yourself to get an idea  of roughly how fast you can be cutting  without too much risk of losing a bunch  of muscle in the process so that is the  video if you liked it please like and  subscribe and if you have a question  that you won't answer tomorrow  ask it in the comments below and if I  like it I will pick it and that is what  tomorrow's video will cover until then  hope your training is great  hope you have a great day and I will see  you tomorrow
 

